  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday through Tuesday)\nIssued at 144 AM EDT Wed Sep 9 2026\n\nThe synoptic pattern over the area will start to shift on Friday,\nas the flow turns out of the southwest with a ridge off to the \neast, and troughing from the OH Valley down to the lower MS\nValley. Moisture will begin to advect up across the area from the\nGulf on Friday, supporting scattered to numerous showers and\nstorms. High temperatures in the low to mid 90s and dewpoints in\nthe low to mid 70s will drive peak heat index values into the\n100-105 range across the area. By Saturday, higher moisture \ncontent (PWATs of 2-2.2\") will spread across the entire area, with\nenhanced forcing from a trough over the TN Valley supporting more\nwidespread showers and storms. While a few storms Friday and\nSaturday could be strong with gusty winds, we could also see a\nthreat for localized flash flooding for any areas that are hit\nwith repeated rounds of storms. Cloud cover may limit how warm \ntemperatures get on Saturday, but generally expecting highs to \nreach the low 90s, with heat index values in the upper 90s to low \n100s.\n\nFrom Sunday through early next week, models are in generally good\nagreement on the mid-level ridge over the Atlantic retrograding\nback west over our area. Moisture content looks to remain above \nnormal (PWATs of 1.75-2\"), but the influence of the ridge would\nkeep shower and thunderstorm coverage isolated to scattered.\nTemperatures and heat index values would remain quite a bit above\nnormal for this time of year, with highs in the low to mid 90s \nand heat index values in the upper 90s to low 100s. At some point\n'fall-like' weather will finally arrive, but its very unlikely \nfor the next 7-10 days.",
